AB  - AI is impacting engineering practice in a variety of ways, including accelerating documentation, analysis, and routine decision support. Recent National Society of Professional Engineers (NSPE) rulings and IEEE transparency standards emphasize that licensed engineers retain non-delegable duties of review, disclosure, and accountability whenever AI contributes to engineering deliverables. The presented work translates NSPE's recent guidance and IEEE standards on transparency into a practical, team-level operating rhythm consisting of three core components: a three-pillar framework (Ethics in Practice, Fit-for-Purpose Risk Assessment, Values Alignment), a clear boundary between AI-capable tasks - also known as complicated tasks (AI-supportable) - and those requiring human intervention - also known as complex tasks (human-critical), and a 90-day leader-led pilot plan with lightweight controls (logs, rubrics, disclosures). The proposed approach emphasizes augmentation over automation, aiming for improved efficiency and consistency while preserving human judgment and public trust. By structuring AI adoption around these professional obligations and transparency requirements, engineering teams can move beyond ad hoc tool use toward a repeatable, accountable system that aligns with licensure duties and risk management best practices. © 1973-2011 IEEE.
