AD  - Dayananda Sagar University, Dept of CSE, SoE, Bengaluru, India
AB  - Artificial Superintelligence (ASI) is a hypothetical idea concerning a level of AI where a machine could outperform humans in every aspect - logic, creativity, knowledge and even emotional empathy. Although there have been considerable advancements in the development of Artificial Narrow Intelligence (ANI) and Artificial General Intelligence (AGI), ASI remains a mere theoretical concept that raises questions not only about technology and ethics but also about the survival of humanity. This study is tuned to the continuum from ANI to AGI and considers various paths leading to ASI. The survey not only deeply analyzes the notions of recursive self-improvement and intelligence explosion hypothesis but also considers different perspectives - computational, quantum, and neuromorphic that might be the future of superintelligent systems. Besides that, it introduces the architectonic models, safety and security provisions, and global governance mechanisms through which the ethical progression of ASI would be achieved, as topics for discussion. This paper, by diversifying the know-how of various disciplines, illuminates the largest unresolved problems such as value alignment, interpretability, and ethical oversight, and sketches a future-oriented roadmap marked by openness and human-centered design. This initiative is designed to develop a common comprehension of the potential benefits and dangers of Artificial Superintelligence (ASI) across researchers, policymakers and tech experts. The lead point of the paper is to make sure that such highly intelligent systems are safe, manageable and, most importantly, consistent with human ethics. © 2026 IEEE.
