AB  - This chapter presents a philosophical expansion of physical literacy (PL) through the lens of Advaita Vedanta, an Indian perspective that emphasises the integrated nature of body, mind, and consciousness. It explores foundational concepts such as non-duality, witnessing, self-realisation, and interoception – positioning movement not merely as a physical skill or cognitive function but as an expression of unified being. A key distinction is drawn between self-actualisation and self-realisation: while the former relates to personal achievement within the bounds of ego and identity, the latter transcends these constructs to uncover a deeper, unchanging self. Within this framework, PL becomes a lifelong process of inner alignment, where movement serves as a medium for embodied awareness, personal transformation, and holistic well-being. Interoception – awareness of internal physiological and energetic states – emerges as central to cultivating this embodied self-awareness. Practical implications for educators include adopting pedagogical approaches that cultivate mindful engagement, emotional resilience, and reflective movement practices. By integrating these philosophical insights into physical education, the chapter proposes a consciousness-informed model of PL that expands traditional domain-based constructs. Rooted in Indian spiritual traditions, this model broadens the conceptual foundations of PL and opens new pathways for inclusive, holistic educational practice. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Liz Durden-Myers; individual chapters, the contributors.
