AD  - Laboratory for Advanced Computing and Intelligence, Information Engineering University, Henan, Zhengzhou, China
AB  - Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) has revolutionized autonomous navigation for Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs), enabling sophisticated path planning and obstacle avoidance capabilities. However, the security vulnerabilities of DRL systems, particularly susceptibility to backdoor attacks, pose significant risks to critical applications. This paper introduces a novel backdoor attack method based on Value Function Shaping (VFS), which subtly manipulates the reward function during training to implant malicious behaviors while maintaining high performance on benign tasks. Unlike traditional backdoor attacks that directly modify actions or flip rewards, VFS operates by introducing a steering coefficient into the discounted reward calculation, allowing the value function to learn malicious associations without significantly altering the policy distribution. Through extensive experiments in a 28 × 28 grid-based UAV navigation environment, it is demonstrated that VFS achieves an Attack Success Rate (ASR) above 99.5% while maintaining 94% Baseline Accuracy Preservation (BAP), outperforming baseline methods including Action Poisoning (AP) and Reward Hacking (RH). Furthermore, VFS exhibits superior stealthiness with an entropy gap of only 0.1608 compared to 1.1971 for AP and 0.8513 for RH, making it significantly harder to detect through standard validation procedures. The findings highlight the critical need for robust defense mechanisms against such covert attacks in DRL-based autonomous systems.  © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
