AD  - Department of Computer Science, Texas Tech University, Lubbock, 79409, TX, United States
AD  - Department of Computer Science and Security, St. Pölten University of Applied Sciences, Saint Pölten, 3100, Austria
AD  - Department of Psychological Sciences, Texas Tech University, Lubbock, 79409, TX, United States
AB  - With the rapid advancement of AI and foundation models, several research directions have emerged which are active research focuses contributing to advancing and stabilizing the next generation of cutting-edge AI technologies. Instances of such research efforts are being explored through various concepts such as AI Alignment, Explainable AI and Transparency, AI Compliance, AI Ethics, Constitutional AI, and notably Controllable AI. Although the needs for all these research concepts and focuses are well-justified, there is little known how these areas contribute to trustworthiness in AI and whether there is any overlap or missing research gaps that need to be explored for addressing the hard-core research problems and concerns in trustworthy AI. Furthermore, it is also primitive to understand how trustworthiness can be addressed in the context of ‘‘agentic AI’’, and more notably ‘‘controllable AI’’ where LLM-powered agents collaboratively reason and decide about workflows and tasks without human’s intervention. The key idea is that while ‘‘agentic AI’’ offers sophisticated technology and paradigm for the next generations of computing, the trustworthiness of such fascinating technology is still in its infancy, and therefore controllability of such agentic AI technology needs to be explored. This conceptual article intends to explore and view the research focuses in AI (e.g., AI Alignment, Explainability) in accordance with AI trustworthiness. More specifically, the work compares and contrasts these conceptual concepts with respect to the NIST Trustworthy AI framework that embodies seven criteria that include safety, security, explainability, privacy, fairness, validity, and accountability. The article provides a conceptual mapping between each of these concepts and the trustworthiness criteria listed in the NIST trustworthy framework. The paper introduces ACT, an Active framework for controlling trustworthiness criteria in AI systems. © 2026 The Authors.
