AB  - This chapter examines the fundamental question of whether the AI control problem is solvable. Despite significant research in AI safety and security, there has been little rigorous analysis of whether controlling highly capable AI systems is achievable even in principle. We provide a definition of the AI control problem and analyze different types of control, focusing on the most challenging case of recursively self-improving superintelligence. Through logical arguments and paradoxes, we demonstrate that complete and safe control of superintelligent AI is impossible without sacrificing either safety or capability. We show that explicit control leads to paradoxes, delegated control results in loss of human values, and intermediate approaches represent an unsatisfactory tradeoff between control and safety. The paper concludes that unrestricted intelligence cannot be controlled, while restricted intelligence cannot outperform human-level capabilities. This work aims to shift the discourse in AI safety research and highlights the critical importance of thoroughly examining fundamental assumptions about AI controllability before pursuing solutions that may prove impossible. © 2026 by John Wiley & Sons, Inc. All rights reserved, including rights for text and data mining and training of artificial intelligence technologies or similar technologies.
