AB  - Large language models (LLMs) have been presented with Trolley problems to analyze their alignment with human choices. The growing presence of autonomous vehicles (AVs) in cities and the imminent arrival of humanoid robots raise the question: What will LLMs decide in life-or-death scenarios involving humans, pets, and robots? We extended the Moral Machine experiments to introduce self-conscious robots (entities aware of their own existence) as a third species, alongside humans and pets, testing six LLMs across 1,000 trolley-problem scenarios. Employing a conjoint analysis, we evaluated the models’ preferences towards each species and other domains. We found that LLMs prioritized humans, then self-conscious robots, and finally, pets. However, the margin preference of humans over robots was narrow. These findings establish a baseline for LLMs’ moral reasoning towards robots and reveal potential misalignments, such as the slight preference for humans over robots. Our results inform AI alignment efforts and AV systems design, as robots become more prevalent in our society. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
