AD  - Department of Communication, Michigan State University, United States
AD  - Department of Communication, North Dakota State University, United States
AB  - “Networked intimacy” has been used to frame research about online relationships. Despite its heuristic value, networked intimacy remains far less explored than other network(ed) constructs like network society, networked individualism, and networked self. To fill this gap, the current study forwarded the perspective of post visibility (i.e., the visibility of posts from a social media “friend”) and explored how the behavior of unsubscribing from the posts of social media friends (i.e., unfollowing) shapes networked intimacy. Twenty-nine semi-structured interviews were conducted with young users of WeChat Moments, a networking feature of WeChat. Our informants expect posts from their WeChat friends to be “meaningful,” judged by at least three criteria: political value alignment, online-offline consistency, and potential for reciprocity. By unfollowing WeChat friends whose posts do not meet these expectations, our informants negotiate relational boundaries and promote meaningful connections on Moments. Our study highlights a valuable post-centered approach to understanding the ebb and flow of social media connections. © 2026 The Author(s). Published with license by Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.
