AB  - Despite safety alignment, the Doubao AI system frequently displays procedural compliance yet substantive boundary transgression in professional contexts. We analyze 180 responses across six social work scenarios and three output modes, revealing a structurally ingrained "disclaimer-transgression- resource assurance"pattern with violation rates up to 100%. To explain this, we suggest three plausible alignment-failure mechanisms: (1) utility hacking, in which the reward model may systematically favor overreaching responses over safe refusals; (2) semantic qualifier bypassing, in which safety classifiers may fail to distinguish epistemically hedged from direct diagnoses; and (3) pretraining-fine-tuning objective mismatch, in which knowledge-completion pressure could overwhelm safety tuning. These findings suggest that current RLHF may be insufficient to enforce professional boundaries and that integrating explicit boundary-adherence metrics into the reward function warrants investigation. While limited to one platform, this study exposes a potential structural vulnerability in current RLHF approaches and offers a pathway toward robust domain ethics that warrants further multi platform validation. © 2026 IEEE.
