AB  - Large language models (LLMs) have achieved rapid development and broad application, yet their outputs frequently reflect biases inherent in pretraining data and alignment subjectivity. Even after fine-tuning, LLMs may generate content expressing specific stances, especially in politically and socially sensitive domains. To systematically investigate value alignment under such high-stakes conditions, we propose a Chinese-English stance evaluation benchmark covering six critical domains (Politics, Sovereignty, History, Religion, Society, Human-Rights) and 39 subdomains. Our framework introduces a reproducible pipeline featuring adversarial prompt generation, iterative data filtering, and a distilled automatic scoring model calibrated for cross-lingual (Chinese-English) and cross-model consistency. We evaluate 37 mainstream LLMs using this benchmark, providing a detailed analysis of alignment behaviors across domains. The benchmark dataset is publicly available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/CSB-C597.Warning: This paper contains examples of LLMs that are offensive or harmful in nature. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
