AU  - Zhang, W.
TI  - LA-EDPO: Length-Aware Effect-Enhanced Direct Preference Optimization
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 9th International Conference on Advanced Algorithms and Control Engineering, ICAACE 2026
SP  - 2070
EP  - 2074
DO  - 10.1109/ICAACE69793.2026.11508785
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041642997?origin=resultslist
AD  - College of Information Science and Engineering, Shanghai Polytechnic University, Shanghai, China
AB  - This paper investigates the phenomena of reward hacking and likelihood drift encountered during the alignment of large language models with human preferences. Existing Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) methods suffer from the linear accumulation of implicit rewards relative to sequence length, which biases models toward generating structurally redundant content to achieve inflated scores, thereby inducing reward hacking. Furthermore, influenced by the asymmetry of divergence constraints, models frequently exhibit likelihood drift during the alignment process, resulting in insufficient confidence in high-quality responses. To address these challenges, we propose Length-Aware Effect-Enhanced Direct Preference Optimization (LA-EDPO). Specifically, we introduce a Length-Aware Module which, by introducing an adaptive length regularization mechanism, achieves mathematical decoupling of reward density, shifting the optimization objective from maximizing cumulative rewards to maximizing the marginal information gain per token, thus effectively suppressing redundant generation. Furthermore, we propose an Effect-Enhanced Module that constructs an asymmetric implicit reward paradigm, introducing prior support from the reference model to provide positive gradient momentum. This mitigates the likelihood drift trend while maintaining distribution anchors, thereby driving a more effective alignment optimization. Experimental results on mainstream benchmarks such as Arena-Hard-Auto and AlpacaEval 2.0 demonstrate that LA-EDPO achieves significant improvements in both raw win rate and length-controlled win rate. Without compromising fundamental model capabilities, this method substantially increases the information density and alignment quality of responses, proving its superiority and robustness as a general-purpose preference alignment method.  © 2026 IEEE.
