AB  - Generative artificial intelligence (AI) has emerged as a powerful tool for creating advertising content with the distinctive capability to generate imaginative and fantasy-oriented scenarios. However, the study of users’ perceptions and responses to AI fantasy generation as a unique affordance remains open. Based on affordance theory and the stimulus-organism-response (SOR) framework, this study proposes the Fantasy Inspiration, Immersion, and Task-AI Alignment (FIT) framework to explain the effect of fantasy affordances in AI-generated advertising on user responses. A 3 × 2 between-subjects factorial design, considering three fantasy types (environmental, entity, and behavioral fantasy) and two levels (high vs. low), was performed with 567 participants. We demonstrate that high fantasy affordances consistently enhance advertising outcomes across all fantasy types. Using partial least squares structural equation modeling (PLS-SEM), we found that fantasy affordances operate entirely through three FIT mechanisms to influence ad engagement and attitude. AI’s fantasy generation capabilities represent a valuable affordance by enhancing users’ perceived task–AI alignment in creative advertising contexts. This research contributes to the human–AI interaction literature by reframing AI from a competitor to human creativity as a complementary tool with distinctive imaginative capabilities. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
