AB  - The proceedings contain 29 papers. The special focus in this conference is on Human-Computer Interaction. The topics include: Evaluating Acceptance of an AI-Based Coaching Chatbot for Virtual Reflection in Interdisciplinary Project Teams; directions for Computational Theory of Mind: Data, Metrics, Models and Mathematical Formalization; Implementation - Stage Human-Centered AI Assessment; Are We Becoming More Critical of AI? Findings From a Two-Year Study of Public Perception, Use Cases, and Sci-Fi’s Role in Shaping AI Views; a Hybrid Recommendation Framework for Enhancing User Engagement in Local News; enhancing Interpretability and Gaining Insights into Robustness in Vision-Language Models Through Core and Spurious Feature Detection via Counterfactuals; a Traffic Risk Semantic Framework for Enhanced Trajectory Prediction and Explainable Risk Assessment in Autonomous Driving; Networked Two-Way Communication Channels (NTCC): A Dynamic Semantic Indexing Framework for UI Design and AI Alignment; fostering Positive Interactions Through Psychological Ownership in Intelligent Systems; Designing for Trustworthiness in AI-Based Fact-Checking Services; Who is the Human in AI Ethics Texts?; Exploring the Role of AI Guidance in Internet-Based Acceptance and Commitment Therapy: Links to Human-Computer Trust and Help-Seeking Barriers; constrained Causal Decision Dilemmas; Cartesian Methodical Doubt: A Cognitive Framework for Reasoning and Explainability in AI Systems; Stress Management Utilizing an AI Mental Health Chatbot in a Trier Social Stress Test Paradigm; understanding the Limitations of Large Language Models in Credibility-Tracking Tasks; Beyond Syntax: Evaluating the Depth, Bias, and Expressiveness of Human vs. AI-Generated Text; Descriptive Assessment of Student Code by LLMs: An Empirical Study; Pre-consultation Medical Assistant: An LLM-Based Support System for Physicians; leveraging a Large Language Model to Enhance Motivation in Learning Programming Languages;