UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037855625?origin=resultslist
AD  - School of Electrical and Computer Engineering, Georgia Institute of Technology, Atlanta, GA, United States
AD  - School of Computer Science, Georgia Institute of Technology, Atlanta, GA, United States
AB  - As artificial intelligence systems become increasingly autonomous and consequential, ensuring they make not merely accurate but genuinely wise decisions has emerged as a critical challenge. This paper presents a framework for Wise AI - systems that integrate short-term and long-term utility optimization with ethical reasoning, safety constraints, and long-term value preservation. Unlike conventional AI that maximizes narrow performance metrics, our framework formalizes stochastic multi-objective optimization with hard ethical constraints, domain-specific operational constraints, epistemic uncertainty quantification, and human-in-the-loop mechanisms. We introduce the wisdom function to balance these objectives subject to inviolable safety constraints, problem-specific resource constraints, and uncertainty thresholds. We propose methods for estimating ethical alignment and long-term value using both domain-specific metrics and reinforcement learning. Through simulation across six diverse scenarios - hospital resource allocation, autonomous vehicle collision avoidance, financial trading, social media content moderation, medical diagnosis, and hiring algorithms - we illustrate how Wise AI can achieve higher wisdom scores while maintaining perfect safety compliance. This work provides a framework for developing AI systems aligned with human values and societal welfare. © 2026 IEEE.
