TI  - A Retrieval-Augmented and Human-Aligned Framework for Trustworthy Medical Question Answering
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 9th International Conference on Advanced Algorithms and Control Engineering, ICAACE 2026
SP  - 2572
EP  - 2576
DO  - 10.1109/ICAACE69793.2026.11508769
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041610577?origin=resultslist
AD  - Monash University, Faculty of Information Technology, Melbourne, 3800, VIC, Australia
AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate significant promise in the medical domain, yet their application is often hindered by factual inaccuracies (hallucinations) and a lack of safety awareness. While Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is a common technique to mitigate these issues by grounding responses in external knowledge, conventional RAG frameworks often suffer from unreliable retrieval and untrustworthy generation, fundamentally lacking alignment with human values. To address this gap, we introduce RAHF-Med (Retrieval-Augmented and Human-Aligned Framework for Medicine), a novel framework that integrates Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) into the RAG pipeline. RAHF-Med first trains a reward model on the preferences of medical experts to evaluate the quality of answer-evidence pairs. This reward model then guides the optimization of a generator policy via an efficient offline method involving best-of-N sampling, re-ranking, and supervised fine-tuning. This process aligns the model with both factual accuracy and crucial expert values, such as prudence and safety. Experimental results demonstrate that RAHF-Med significantly outperforms a baseline RAG model, achieving a reward score of 0.470 and a faithfulness score of 0.971, in stark contrast to the baseline's 0.002. These results indicate a substantial improvement in generating professional, reliable, and trustworthy answers, even when presented with noisy or contaminated retrieved contexts. Our work introduces a new paradigm for developing dependable medical AI systems by aligning them with both factual evidence and human values.  © 2026 IEEE.
