AB  - This chapter examines the institutional challenges and governance gaps in applying blockchain to land administration, where much of the literature has overstated technological attributes such as immutability, transparency, and efficiency while under examining legal enforceability, institutional fit, and governance sustainability. Building on Baird’s (1998) four-element framework, previously applied to land systems by Steudler et al. (2004), authors delimit the scope to two elements most salient for institutional appraisal: implementation strategy and results with monitorable indicators. Authors links implementation steps with verifiable indicators —timeliness to legal effect, institutional coherence, and corrigibility— anchored to statutory milestones and designed for third-party audit. Rather than presupposing net benefits, the study offers evidence-based criteria to assess whether blockchain reforms merely compress frictions or deliver substantive institutional improvement in adjudication, registration, transaction processing, and oversight. Copyright © 2026 by IGI Global Scientific Publishing. All rights reserved. No part of this publication may be reproduced,
