AB  - Security robots increasingly assume authoritative roles, but the underlying mechanisms for why humans cooperate with them is not well understood. This study proposed and tested a cooperation model based on legitimacy theory, focusing on how distributive fairness (outcome equity) and interactional fairness (treatment equity) influence robot legitimacy and cooperation. Using a 2 × 2 online video-based experiment with 372 U.S. participants, the authors found that both fairness types promote cooperation through value alignment, with a non-significant path through obligation to obey; meanwhile, perceived effectiveness was strongly associated with both value alignment and obligation to obey. These findings extend legitimacy theory to human-robot interaction in a U.S. context, emphasizing fairness and perceived effectiveness as key to fostering cooperation and informing ethical robot design.  © 2026 Owner/Author.
