AD  - United States
AB  - Cloud platforms are undergoing a structural transition as artificial intelligence (AI) agents emerge as intelligent intermediaries capable of autonomously operating software systems. Traditional Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) subscription models rely heavily on human-driven interactions, where users manually access features, initiate workflows, and navigate interfaces. This results in inconsistent usage patterns, high underutilization of subscription tiers, and weak alignment between actual consumption and subscription pricing. To address these limitations, this study introduces an AI-Agent Orchestrated Cloud Interaction Model designed to demonstrate how autonomous agents fundamentally reshape operational and economic behaviour within SaaS environments. A publicly available SaaS Subscription Analytics Dataset was employed to capture real-world user activity. After structured preprocessing, usage attribute extraction was performed to derive operational indicators such as workflow density, automation potential, and usage-to-cost ratios. Two contrasting scenarios were then constructed: a baseline traditional subscription model and an AI-agent-intermediated model. The proposed multi-agent orchestration layer was used to simulate autonomous API-level interactions, generate agent-driven usage patterns, and model behavioural changes under automated consumption. Cost modelling demonstrated a clear transition from fixed, underutilized subscription fees to granular, actionbased micro-transactions, improving cost-value alignment for users while increasing workload-correlated revenue for providers. Performance evaluation showed substantial gains, with workflow throughput becoming five to eight times higher, automation density reaching 70-95%, error rates approaching zero, and significantly broader feature utilization. These findings confirm that AI agents not only enhance operational efficiency but also disrupt traditional SaaS economics, establishing a scalable, automation-centric paradigm for nextgeneration cloud platforms. © 2026 IEEE.
