AD  - Electrical Engineering and Computer Science, York University, Toronto, ON, Canada
AD  - Department of Computer Science, University of Ghana, Greater Accra, Legon, Ghana
AB  - Immersive technologies, particularly head-mounted virtual reality (VR), are increasingly used to support preventive health behaviours, but the user-modelling and adaptation mechanisms underpinning personalisation are inconsistently reported and difficult to compare across studies. To brige this gap, a PRISMA-guided systematic review was conducted across six databases (ACM Digital Library, IEEE Xplore, Scopus, PubMed/MEDLINE, CINAHL, and APA PsycINFO) covering studies published from 2010 onward. The search returned 6,489 records; after deduplication, 6,333 unique records were screened; 103 reports were sought for retrieval; 90 reports were retrieved and assessed for eligibility, out of which 27 studies met the inclusion criteria. Within the retrieved corpus, personalisation was predominantly implemented through static tailoring, segment-level targeting, or predefined variants; no included study reported adaptive user modelling or closed-loop updating. Outcomes were primarily assessed as immediate intention and affective responses, whereas behavioural uptake measures and longer follow-up windows were uncommon, constraining inference about sustained preventive action. Cultural tailoring was rarely reported in sufficient detail to evaluate value-alignment and norm-alignment; only one out of the 27 studies reported value-aligned and norm-aligned design, while most others were limited to translation or surface localisation. Two synthesis artefacts are presented: (i) a pipeline taxonomy linking user signals, user representations, adaptation loci, persuasive strategies, and outcomes, and (ii) an evidence map summarising outcome coverage, reported effect direction (not pooled effect sizes), and follow-up windows by preventive target. Overall, adaptation effects were rarely isolated from immersion and content effects, underscoring the need for targeted evaluation designs and deeper contextual modelling to support defensible claims about personalisation benefits.  © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
