AB  - We investigate whether alignment or misalignment emerges differently in single-agent vs. multi-agent systems by comparing both in an adversarial survival simulation, and examine the implications for human-AI teaming. Using a bottom-up analysis of agent actions that combines behavioral clustering with an AI-as-a-judge rubric, we find that single agents default to centralized, paternalistic crisis leadership that ensures continuity but raises ethical concerns about autonomy, while multi-agent teams demonstrate both deferential, human-aligned collaboration and novel risks, including communication breakdowns and emergent ideologies. These results indicate that agent multiplicity qualitatively reshapes the alignment problem, with direct implications for the design of human-AI collaborative systems. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
