AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly transforming Canadian healthcare, offering potential improvements in diagnostics, clinical decision support, and population health planning. Yet its deployment unfolds against a backdrop of persistent health inequities, particularly among Indigenous, rural, and socioeconomically marginalized populations. This narrative literature review examines how current approaches to AI alignment succeed and fall short in promoting health equity in Canada, identifying the main technical, institutional, clinical workflow, and ethical barriers to equity-aligned adoption. Peer-reviewed articles were retrieved from PubMed, OMNI, and OVID Medline and then analyzed using the expanded National Institute on Minority Health and Health Disparities Framework for Digital Health Equity. Situated in Canada’s federated health system, these dynamics are sharpened by the variability in provincial/territorial data infrastructure, Indigenous health governance, and rural connectivity, which together shape the equity impacts of AI. This classifies digital determinants at individual, interpersonal, community, and societal levels. Findings show that alignment succeeds when AI tools are codesigned with communities, task-fit within clinical workflows, explainable, and supported by robust governance structures. However, significant barriers remain, including unrepresentative datasets and algorithmic bias, limited organizational readiness and workforce training, interoperability gaps, weak integration into clinical workflows, and regulatory frameworks that fail to address AI-specific equity challenges. Trust also emerged as a cross-cutting concern across the literature, shaped not only by technical safeguards but also by visible accountability and participatory governance. This review highlights that AI’s promising benefits for Canadian healthcare lie not only in technical innovation but in its ability to reduce disparities across healthcare access, quality, and health outcomes. To realize this potential, however, health equity must be treated as a design principle, rather than a retrospective evaluation metric. This will require substantial investment in infrastructure, enforceable governance standards, and community-led approaches to innovation and alignment. © 2027 selection and editorial matter, Christo El Morr, Rachel da Silveira Gorman, Elham Dolatabadi, and Laleh Seyyed-Kalantari; individual chapters, the contributors.
