AD  - KPR Institute of Engineering and Technology, Department of ECE, Coimbatore, India
AB  - Wireless charging efficiency in electric vehicles (EVs) relies on proper alignment of the vehicle's wireless receiver to its wireless power transfer charging pad, which has a significant impact on the performance and safety of an EV's wireless power transfer, as misalignment will cause the amount of power transferred from the charger to the EV to be diminished and increase safety risks. This paper presents a smart wireless charging system for EVs using artificial intelligence alignment verification, RFID-based verification for authentication, and real-time IoT-based monitoring. The ESP32-CAM camera is used to provide images of the charging area of the vehicle, and the system's on-board AI utilizes a lightweight edge shift convolution neural network (ESCNN Lite) that implements real-time binary classification on embedded hardware using spatial shift operations and depthwise separable convolutions (DWSC) to reduce computational complexity. An electric vehicle is able to charge once it has completed the RFID-based authentication to the charger and has been confirmed as aligned with the charger through successful alignment detection. Finally, the system will monitor for any misalignment or vehicle departure. It will automatically stop charging if either condition occurs, or the system will send all system information to a cloud dashboard for remote monitoring of operation. The experimental validation shows that the model provides reliable alignment detection with low memory usage and low inference latency, making it appropriate for deployment in resource-limited smart EV charging systems. © 2026 IEEE.
