AB  - Marital happiness, a critical determinant of marriage rates, can be analyzed through computational modeling rather than relying solely on theoretical or empirical approaches. In this study, marriage is modeled as a repeated Stag Hunt game and simulated using multi-agent reinforcement learning. Specifically, we formulate a marital game where stag hunting corresponds to "high effort"and hare hunting to "low effort", and equilibrium strategies are analyzed within this framework. To capture endogenous psychological dynamics, two state variables, "trust degree"and "value alignment degree", are introduced and quantitatively modeled. A dual-agent Q-learning algorithm with experience replay is then implemented to conduct large-scale simulations of marital interaction dynamics. Results show that even from unfavorable initial states (low trust, low value alignment), agents gradually overcome the coordination dilemma, converging toward cooperative equilibria. The computational experiments demonstrate that our modeling framework can effectively simulate the evolution from low-happiness to high-happiness marriages, achieving a mutual high-effort cooperation rate of 72.8%. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
