AB  - This chapter investigates how diversity, inclusion, and an open mindset (DIO) foster organizational resilience and innovation in response to external shocks, drawing on a mixed-methods case study of New Oriental Education, a leading private educational enterprise in China. Through interviews and surveys with leaders and employees, the research explores how DIO values were embedded in organizational culture and leadership practices, enabling adaptive responses to government policy reforms. The study also examines the integration of intercultural leadership – blending Chinese and Western philosophies – as a critical force shaping change. Findings highlight the role of collective identity, psychological safety, and value alignment in promoting globally competent leadership for inclusive governance. Positioned within the context of Globalization 4.1, this chapter contributes to global leadership discourse by offering a culturally grounded framework for navigating complexity while sustaining cohesion, adaptability, and ethical direction. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Michael Guo-Brennan, Linyuan Guo-Brennan, Maria Guajardo and Khalid Arar; individual chapters, the contributors. All rights reserved.
