AD  - Lecturer in Marketing, Bocconi University, Via Roentgen 1, 20136 Milan, Italy
AB  - This paper explores how entrepreneurial ventures can sustainably innovate business models in B2B contexts with structural constraints, such as the Cultural and Creative Industries (CCIs). Drawing on the complex offerings concept in the B2B literature and a single-case study of an opera festival devoted to rediscovering forgotten composers – Il Belcanto Ritrovato (IBR) – we adopt an effectuation lens to unpack how ventures manage four core tensions that emerge from five types of constraints: symbolic, institutional, resource-based, knowledge-based, and governance. Our findings identify four resolution mechanisms – selective knowledge brokerage, boundary-spanning positioning, targeted value alignment, and latent asset mobilization – through which viable business models can be constructed. These mechanisms reflect distinct combinations of formal and informal relational arrangements and illustrate a process termed as effectual relational crafting , whereby business models emerge through dynamic stakeholder configuration rather than linear planning. The study contributes to the B2B marketing, business model innovation, and effectuation literature by demonstrating how innovation under constraint unfolds through adaptive, means-driven stakeholder engagement. © 2026 Elsevier Inc.
