AD  - Exl, Florence Ave, United States
AD  - The University of Texas at Arlington, Dept of Computer Science, Arlington, TX, United States
AB  - This faster progress in the direction of Artificial General Intelligence (AGI) has catalyzed the emergence of the point of ethical regulation of it, particularly when it is combined with the cyber-physical systems (CPS), which are directly tied to the physical world. The existing effectual and regulatory systems tend to dwell more on the upper-tier ethical values such as transparency, accountability, and fairness but do not dwell on the enforcement methodology, the ongoing validation, and the comprehensive system incorporation into safety-intensive CPS architectures. Moreover, the current governance systems remain still patchy technically, legally, and policy-wise, which does not offer much-desired guidance as to how to deal with self-directed and self-driven AGI behaviors. The research paper seals these gaps by devising one ethical system of governance that balances the ethical factors to CPS engineering and operational requirements. The framework suggests measurable indicators, lifecycle management, and multilayered system monitors to enhance the correspondence, security, and responsibility of the system in evolution. It is hoped that the suggested remedy might boost the degree of trust and strength of AGI-enabled CPS since ethical limitations are introduced during the system design, runtime verification, and post-implementation validation. The findings of this study would assist in providing the foundations of a scalable, enforceable, and context-specific ethical regulation upon future AGI applications to cyber-physical space. © 2026 IEEE.
