AB  - This study adopts a longitudinal narrative inquiry to examine how two experienced Chinese university EFL teachers’ professional identities evolved during a year-long process of AI integration in English teaching. Drawing on Clandinin and Connelly’s three-dimensional narrative inquiry framework, the study collected narrative data from in-depth interviews, biweekly reflective journals, and human-AI interaction logs. Thematic narrative analysis revealed distinct trajectories: Li, leveraging an applied linguistics background, evolved into a technology-enhanced pedagogy innovator, while Zhang, grounded in literature and humanistic values, developed into a cautious, selective integrator of AI and a trusted peer advisor. Their identity evolution unfolded through iterative cycles of experimentation, reflection, and value alignment; it was mediated by interpretive resources, contextual affordances, technological agency, and agentic enactment. Building on the findings, the study proposes the Identity Evolution in AI-Mediated Teaching (IEAT) model, offering a conceptual framework to understand teacher identity evolution in AI-enhanced contexts. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
