AB  - On Chinese social media, “high emotional intelligence” (高情商, High EQ) has emerged as a folk category for discussing socially skillful responses to awkward or face-threatening situations. These discussions form a naturalistic site for studying pragmatic judgment as a socially negotiated practice. This paper examines how understandings of High EQ are shared among human users and the extent to which large language models (LLMs) align with human evaluations. Using a corpus of 40 RedNote discussion posts and their associated responses, we compared human and LLM evaluations on High EQ responses along three dimensions: perceived High EQ, aggressiveness, and expected popularity. Combining quantitative agreement analysis with qualitative analysis informed by speech-act and politeness theory, we found that humans exhibit strong consensus on what counts as High EQ and aggressiveness, but little agreement on popularity. LLM judgments diverge systematically from human judgments, particularly for High EQ responses that rely on indirect off-record facework. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
