AB  - High obstacles in complex natural environments severely limit robot mobility. For heavy wheeled-legged robots, high-obstacle climbing is fundamentally more difficult than on lightweight platforms because of their larger mass and inertia, amplified modeling mismatch, and higher tendency toward actuator saturation. This paper presents a deployment-oriented safe reinforcement learning (SRL) framework for 1 m vertical obstacle climbing on a 340 kg wheeled-legged robot. High-obstacle climbing is formulated as a constrained RL problem, and constraint costs are introduced with multi-head cost critics in an asymmetric actor–critic architecture to optimize reward and constraints in parallel. A task-specific early-termination rule is introduced to suppress reward-hacking behaviors during training. The learned controller is deployed zero-shot on the 340 kg platform and achieves stable 1 m vertical obstacle climbing. To the best of our knowledge, this work provides one of the first system-level demonstrations of constrained RL for 1 m vertical obstacle climbing on a 340 kg-class wheeled-legged robot. Experimental results verify the effectiveness and real-world deployability of the proposed method under high dynamic constraints, supporting the application of RL to extreme maneuver control of heavy wheeled-legged robots. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
