AB  - Feedback and assessment remain central to both student learning and institutional accountability within higher education. Despite the extensive development of feedback frameworks within higher education, their capacity to support continuous improvement at the institutional level remains uncertain. This paper presents a systematic review of feedback generation frameworks and models, with more attention on engineering education and artificial intelligence (AI) enhanced practices. The review employs an agility lens (defined through the dimensions of iteration, responsiveness, value alignment, transparency, and efficiency), to evaluate how feedback frameworks and models contribute to institutional learning. Data was collected from Web of Science database (2000-2025) using seven search strings, yielding Thirty-five records after initial filtering. Following title and abstract screening, twenty-three studies were included for full analysis. Results indicate that pedagogical frameworks demonstrate strong iteration and responsiveness but limited institutional alignment, while AI-driven approaches show efficiency but insufficient transparency and integration into leadership processes. Institutional frameworks emphasise accountability and value alignment but lack agility. The study concludes that agility measures can provide a critical benchmark for assessing the effectiveness of feedback systems and recommends embedding agile feedback loops across institutional quality cycles to bridge the gap between teaching-level feedback and organisational improvement. © 2026 IEEE.
