AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) systems increasingly make decisions that require human trust, yet little is known about how users evaluate these decisions when moral reasoning is involved. This study examines how different AI reasoning styles–utilitarian, deontological, and virtue-based reasoning–shape user trust and agreement with AI decisions across everyday, corporate, environmental, and medical contexts. In a controlled experiment (N = 160), participants rated AI decisions before and after viewing the system’s explanations. Quantitative analysis revealed that explanations significantly increased trust and agreement, with deontological reasoning yielding the largest gains in higher-stake contexts, while utilitarian reasoning showed weaker effects in medical scenarios. A mediation analysis indicated that perceived transparency partially explained the trust increase, with the remainder attributable to value alignment. We propose a context-sensitive framework for designing explanations that calibrate trust by matching reasoning style to domain expectations and decision stakes. © 2026 The Author(s). Published by Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.
