DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2026.133457
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105043730898?origin=resultslist
AD  - Communication University of China, Beijing, 100024, China
AB  - Understanding human values is a crucial capability for AI systems to promote social norms and align with ethical principles. While prior work focused on language or static images, videos embed rich, dynamic cues through actions, interactions, and temporal context, making value inference more challenging. We study video-based value understanding in multimodal large language models (MLLMs) and identify two main challenges: aligning video content with abstract human values, and ensuring high-quality, diverse training under imbalanced value distributions. To address these, we propose MMVUF, a value-grounded post-training framework with a two-stage paradigm: the Multimodal Alignment Stage generates value-aware textual representations from video, and the Value Alignment Stage maps these representations to human value dimensions while preserving visual grounding through parameter-level inheritance. Additionally, we introduce VSDS, a value-specific data selection algorithm that emphasizes representative and diverse samples through value density weighting and structured sampling. Experiments on the VVALUES dataset show that MMVUF significantly enhances MLLM performance in video value understanding, highlighting the importance of structured alignment and value-aware data selection. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
