AD  - University of Waikato, New Zealand
AB  - Authenticity, defined as the sense of being true to oneself, may function as a psychological resource that supports well-being and work outcomes. Across three studies, we examined how authenticity relates to values alignment and organisational attitudes. A pilot study ( N = 79) provided preliminary evidence that values misalignment was associated with lower authenticity in the work context specifically, informing the design of the main studies. Study 1 ( N = 285) found that state authenticity and values alignment were independently associated with job satisfaction, commitment, belonging, and lower turnover intentions, whereas trait authenticity was not. Study 2 ( N = 180) tested a causal link: manipulation of state authenticity led to increased values alignment. These findings position authenticity as a dynamic psychological resource, highlighting its malleability and positive contribution to personal and work outcomes. © 2026 The Authors.
