AB  - Value alignment between AI systems and users remains a critical challenge in Human-AI Interaction. While data annotation serves as the foundational mechanism for embedding values into AI, alignment issues frequently arise from the cultural disconnect between the standard of expert knowledge and general user perception. Through a comparative study on “patriotism” annotation involving the Expert Group, Lay Group, and Large Language Models, we observed that experts unexpectedly achieved lower accuracy than LLMs. While they slightly outperformed the Lay Group, the margin was minimal, and significant differences in value classification strategies were evident. These results suggest that expert knowledge does not translate directly into annotation accuracy. Instead, Expertise Drift creates a disconnect between expert and lay values, revealing the limitations of the traditional expert-centric “gold standard”. Conversely, the complete absence of expert knowledge introduces cognitive biases in lay annotations, which similarly hinders alignment. Consequently, this study manifests the existence of a profound Epistemological Gap between expert and lay perspectives. To address this, data annotation for LLMs must avoid over-reliance on either extreme. By balancing Normative and Descriptive Logics, the development of AI systems can possess both educational value and cultural inclusivity. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
