AB  - In the research of autonomous driving driven by reinforcement learning, the design of the reward function has always been a core challenge affecting the performance of the model. Traditional reward functions often struggle to balance safety, efficiency, and comfort simultaneously, and are prone to "reward hacking"due to design biases, thereby reducing the robustness and generalization ability of the model. To address this issue, this paper proposes a hierarchical reward function framework for autonomous driving, consisting of a safety layer, an efficiency layer, a comfort layer, and a base layer: The safety layer strictly punishes collision and violation behaviors to ensure basic survival and task completion; the efficiency layer is oriented towards reasonable speed to improve traffic efficiency; the comfort layer suppresses sudden acceleration, braking, and turning to enhance the riding experience; the base layer provides continuous positive driving rewards to ensure stability during the exploration phase. Systematic experiments were conducted in the MetaDrive simulation platform, and the results show that compared with the baseline method, this framework significantly improves the task success rate (from 32% to 49%), while reducing the collision rate from 46% to 26%, effectively achieving a balance between safety, efficiency, and comfort. This study provides a feasible approach for the design of reward functions in complex traffic environments and verifies the advantages of the hierarchical mechanism in enhancing the overall performance of autonomous driving agents. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
