AB  - The purpose of the study is to take a critical view and reflect on the historical development and modern relevance of Environmental, Social, and Governance (ESG) investing, specifically its behavioral biases and its regulatory development. The main aim of this study is to provide a conceptual framework that represents how ESG components are being integrated into investment decisions. Using a qualitative research approach, this study combines historical literature, policy dynamics, and theoretical arguments augmented by the scholars of behavioral finance. It is observed that ESG investing has moved beyond normative ethics-driven sensitivity to performance-guided integration, which is becoming more influenced by psychological and cognitive processes such as moral salience, value alignment, and long-term perceived risk mitigation. The current trends toward the use of ESG in capital markets since the financial crisis of 2008 and international agreements, such as the Kyoto Protocol and the Paris Agreement, have also enhanced this mainstreaming process. The results support the growing concern of regulators in the ESG disclosures as a tactical tool toward countering greenwashing and enhancing the trustworthiness and clarity of the information associated with investments. In addition to the dynamic literature on sustainable finance and behavioral economics, the study is able to provide a refined perspective concerning the dual nature of ESG as a strategic investment metric and an agent of ethical financial behavior guidance. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Kirti Sood, Vikas Sharma, Andreia de Bem Machado; individual chapters, the contributors.
