AD  - Queensland Region, Centre for Appropriate Technology, Alice Springs, Australia
AB  - Co-design literature typically focuses on community participation but less is written about technical partners. The Olkola Cultural Knowledge Centre is a community-led co-design project in Cape York, Australia with architectural, engineering and building pro bono and voluntary technical partnerships. While we have written extensively elsewhere about the Indigenous-led design approach and community particiation in the project, the role of technical partnerships has yet to be explored despite their critical role in realising the project. In 2024 after collaborating for 5 years, it is timely to reflect on why technical partners become and stay involved. This analysis finds technical partners are involved to 1) connect with Indigenous Peoples, 2) realise alternative design practice and 3) build professional development skills. Technical partners stay involved due to 1) an inclusive team/culture, 2) project values alignment and 4) cultural responsibility and community connection. These results inform a new partnership framework for interdisciplinary community-led co-design to optimise sustained technical partner participation alongside the community. © 2025 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.
