AB  - This paper proposes a user-centered modular Artificial Intelligence (AI) architecture designed to enhance alignment accuracy in Augmented Reality Head-Up Displays (AR HUDs) for autonomous driving environments. Misalignment and latency between virtual content and real-world objects in AR HUDs can lead to cognitive delays and visual discomfort, ultimately lowering user satisfaction and causing physical fatigue. To address this, the proposed system divides on-device AI processing into three real-time modules: (1) perceptual state analysis, (2) spatiotemporal alignment correction, and (3) user feedback-driven adaptive content control. Each module reflects real-time user feedback, dynamically adjusting alignment according to contextual awareness, multisensory inputs, and perceptual thresholds. This human-centered design reduces visual latency, mitigates disorientation and fatigue, and enhances information delivery during driving, thereby ensuring safety. As a methodological basis, a user scenario-based approach is adopted, shifting the paradigm performance-centered AR design to predictive, perception-driven AI-human interaction models.  © 2025 IEEE.
