AD  - Lee Kong Chian Faculty of Engineering and Science, Kolej Universiti Tunku Abdul Rahman, Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia
AD  - Oxford Roadmapping, Guangzhou, China
AB  - Both AI professionals and public and private leaders recognize the growing importance of artificial intelligence (AI)-driven innovations and social changes particularly the design, impact and deployment of the Large Language Models (LLMs) across industries and societies. This study employs a technology roadmapping exercise using scientometric analysis based on the Web of Science (WoS) database. Drawing upon a dataset of 188 scholarly publications, a series of science mapping visualizations and tables provides both an overarching perspective and detailed insights into the domain. While academic contributions to this domain have expanded significantly, especially in 2023-2024, it is largely concentrated in institutions in North America and Europe, with notable contributions by the University of California System and Google. Conceptually, it is loosely-coupled. Nonetheless, it spans several disciplines-including computer science, management, ethics, and philosophy-highlighting its inherently cross- and inter-disciplinary nature. The historiograph mapping of the more closely-cited direct network reveals three distinct clusters: (1) the early theoretical work on AI alignment and AI safety, (2) the emerging Large-Language Models (LLMs) research, and (3) CEO activism and stakeholder alignment. Furthermore, the findings provide a performance overview, as the basis for the future technology roadmapping innovation pathways. The scientometric analysis underscores the imperative of addressing both AI misalignment and stakeholder misalignment issues to ensure ethical and safe applications of LLMs. Within the broader systems thinking framework, future work can begin conceptualize an overarching value-alignment mapping that considers the value expressions and specifications of relevant diverse stakeholders for transparent alignment, especially in the context of LLM digital innovation ecosystems. © 2025 IEEE.
