AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is relevant to both the offensive side of cybersecurity (attacking systems) and the defensive side of cybersecurity (detecting or responding to such attacks). On the offensive side, AI enables hackers to more effectively target both machines (by identifying and exploiting vulnerabilities) and humans (through social engineering attacks). But modern AI technology possesses unique weaknesses—fragility, specification gaming, and opacity—that make AI systems themselves vulnerable to attack. Where hackers use AI to access, damage, or manipulate computer systems without authorization or to attack AI systems themselves, such actions are generally punishable under computer misuse statutes and, in the case of AI-based social engineering attacks, laws against fraud. On the defensive side, AI enables system owners operators to better detect and defend against cyberattacks. AI-based defense systems may use deep learning to identify previously unknown threats or may be empowered to automatically respond to active attacks without human intervention. © The Editors and Contributors Severally 2025. All rights reserved.
