AB  - The paradigm of "thinking with images", where Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) act as visual agents, has shown remarkable promise in tackling complex visual reasoning tasks. This approach leverages Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR), enabling models to make multi-round function calls to refine their visual input. However, current RLVR implementations only consider the correctness of the final answers, neglecting the validity of the reasoning process. This can lead to "reward hacking", where models receive high rewards for correct answers despite wrong Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning processes. In this paper, Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Process-supervised Rewards (RLVPR) is proposed to mitigate reward hacking in "thinking with images"MLLMs. RLVPR incorporates a verifiable process-supervised reward system that provides process-level supervision for the model's actions throughout multi-round rollouts. Furthermore, a "function call balance"mechanism is introduced to penalize superfluous function calls, ensuring that the model only takes actions that are instrumental in solving the task. The experimental results show that this fine-grained process supervision leads to substantial performance gains on high-resolution visual-search benchmarks, offering a more robust and reliable path for multimodal reasoning. © 2025 IEEE.
