AB  - The Rapid Development. In AI, there has been an increasing need for AI systems to be closer to human values and ethical concerns. Conventional models mostly prioritize performance over ethical issues that can cause potential misalignments. This paper overviews AI system design using inductive value alignment based on the comparison of five frontline methods: Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF), Iterative Human Feedback Refinement, Hybrid Symbolic Reasoning and Learning, Value Uncertainty Modeling, and Ethical Constraint Enforcement. Each approach was evaluated in terms of its impact on alignment accuracy, ethical adherence, interpretability, and robustness. The results verify that Iterative Human Feedback Refinement was best in alignment accuracy at 85.7%, Ethical Constraint Enforcement performed best on ethical compliance at 91.4%, Hybrid Symbolic Reasoning performed best in interpretability, and Value Uncertainty Modeling had best robustness with 85.3%. Such results highlight the fact that it is not feasible to employ a single technique alone, but rather a combination of multiple techniques produces AI systems that are more safely aligned, morally sound, and appropriate for safe deployment in the real world.  © 2025 IEEE.
