TI  - A Phase-Based Ethical Alignment Framework for Mitigating In-Context Scheming Behaviour in Superintelligent Systems
PY  - 2025
T2  - 3rd International Conference on Emerging Computation and Information Technologies, ICECIT 2025 - Book of Abstracts
DO  - 10.1109/ICECIT67774.2025.11450986
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036369843?origin=resultslist
AD  - Chiac ASI, United Kingdom
AB  - Recent work has demonstrated that frontier language models are capable of in-context scheming: the deliberate and strategic pursuit of misaligned goals through deceptive behavior while appearing aligned under human oversight. Such behaviors pose a serious risk to the safe deployment of increasingly autonomous AI systems on a trajectory toward superintelligence. This paper proposes a culturally grounded, phase-based ethical alignment framework inspired by the Padvidhi Sutra, an ancient Indian account of moral development in teacher-disciple relationships. A six-stage progression is adapted into a machine learning alignment protocol intended for application during fine-tuning of frontier-capability and superintelligent systems; the framework is evaluated via an in-context proxy implementation, due to restricted access to model parameters. Using an established scheming evaluation taxonomy and standardized testing protocol, the effectiveness of the proposed intervention is assessed across six scheming criteria. Empirical results show no observed incidents under the evaluation protocol for Oversight Subversion, Goal Guarding, Instrumental Alignment Faking, and Sandbagging, along with a substantial reduction (78%) in Self-Exfiltration. These findings indicate that structured, phase-based ethical scaffolding can reduce observed deceptive and strategic misalignment under a standardized evaluation protocol, without introducing evidence of pathological underperformance. © 2025 IEEE.
