AB  - This conclusion affirms that spiritual leadership is not a managerial trend but a way of being rooted in inner alignment, deep listening, and ethical coherence. It brings together diverse perspectives (meditation, presence, ethical responsibility, and embodied wisdom) to show that true transformation is existential rather than institutional. A four-part compass frames the path: personal transformation, anchoring practices, embodied tools, and action rooted in service. Rather than offering fixed models, it invites leaders to walk with humility, courage, and clarity. Leadership, in this view, arises not from authority but from presence and marks the beginning of a deeper, ongoing journey. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Catherine Voynnet Fourboul, Marie Holm and Mohammed Raei; individual chapters, the contributors.
