AD  - Al Ain University, College of Business, United Arab Emirates
AB  - This study investigates the compensation preferences of Generation Z in the United Arab Emirates (UAE), a demographic that is rapidly becoming a dominant force in the workforce. Using a structured quantitative survey, data was collected from 365 respondents born between 1997 and 2012 to examine their salary expectations, benefit priorities, and trade-off tendencies. While competitive salary remains the top priority, 89% of participants indicated a strong preference for health insurance, 72% favored flexible working hours, and 52% were willing to accept a lower-than-expected salary in exchange for career growth opportunities. The study results reflect a nuanced shift from traditional financial incentives toward personal well-being, autonomy, and value alignment. Gender and employment status also influenced preferences, with female respondents placing greater importance on work-life balance. The findings underscore that Generation Z is not solely financially motivated but also driven by holistic, value-based considerations. This research provides actionable insights for HR professionals and employers seeking to attract and retain Gen Z talent within the UAE's unique, tax-free yet high-cost labor market. © 2025 IEEE.
