AD  - RTX Technology Research Center, East Hartford, CT, United States
AD  - Raytheon | an RTX Business, Tuscon, AZ, United States
AB  - Advances in machine learning are enabling autonomous systems to operate in environments of increasing complexity, including scenarios with ethical concerns. For many artificial intelligence (AI) systems, decisions are driven by the goal to maximize reward. Policies may contain unintended consequences, which is known as reward hacking. The AI system is optimizing within the constraints defined by the domain and goals and does not have the capability to distinguish between benign and negative consequences beyond specifications. This chapter describes an ongoing effort to develop an application-agnostic framework for AI systems to simulate actions, characterize potential outcomes, and perform introspection to articulate the motivations for action. Such a framework provides the foundational work for higher-level ethical reasoning using consequential and deontological ethics. This enables metrics from consequential ethics to be used to assign ethical value of actions based on outcomes. Simultaneously, metrics from deontological ethics can be applied to evaluate the universality of its motivations. A trolley problem-inspired Navy scenario is used to operationalize and demonstrate this framework. © 2024 Peggy Wu, Hsin-Fu Wu, Brett Israelsen, Robert Grabowski and Kunal Srivastava Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
