AB  - International development organisations face a challenge to reconcile their commitments to universal human rights agendas with the growing expectation to localise and decolonise their approaches in order to shift power to local actors. This article draws on research into children’s rights implementation in Vietnam, specifically the introduction of the national child protection system, to highlight the challenges for international organisations in balancing their roles as creators and proponents of human rights agendas with their aspirations to enable locally driven action. Greater attention to values alignment, elevation of local knowledge, resourcing of intermediaries and a deeper understanding of the agency of local actors on international organisations are suggested as ways to help address the challenges. © 2021 Australian Journal of Human Rights.
