AB  - In this article, we examine the learning performance of various strategies under different conditions using the Voronoi Q-value element (VQE) based on reward in a single-agent environment, and decide how to act in a certain state. In order to test our hypotheses, we performed computational experiments using several situations such as various angles of rotation of VQEs which are arranged into a lattice structure, various angles of an agent's action rotation that has 4 actions, and a random arrangement of VQEs to correctly evaluate the optimal Q-values for state and action pairs in order to deal with continuous-valued inputs. As a result, the learning performance changes when the angle of VQEs and the angle of action are changed by a specific relative position. © 2012 International Symposium on Artificial Life and Robotics (ISAROB).
